(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-chronic-vision-loss-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Chronic Vision Loss Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A mother asks when her daughter's amblyopia must be treated by. What is true?","choices":["There is no time limit","Only in the first year of life","Before seven, and under five is better","Any time before adulthood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Starting earlier gives the better outcome. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man asks why his eyes are screened when nothing hurts. Why is open-angle glaucoma screened for?","choices":["It presents with sudden vision loss","It always follows an injury","It is asymptomatic at onset","It causes severe pain early"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the damage it does cannot be reversed. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has an iris lesion. Which dimensions raise concern for melanoma?","choices":["Only lesions over 10 mm","Over 3 mm across and over 1 mm deep","Under 1 mm across","Any size, if bilateral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nevus is usually smaller and flat. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is diagnosed with idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Which drug is started promptly?","choices":["Acetazolamide","Timolol drops","High-dose oral steroids","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside a supervised weight reduction programme. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with obesity has months of intractable headache, pulsatile tinnitus and brief greying of vision. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Cluster headache","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The demographic and the triad of headache, pulsatile tinnitus and transient visual loss are characteristic. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with uveal melanoma asks where it would spread. Which site is commonest?","choices":["The liver","The brain","The contralateral eye","The lungs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and surveillance is directed there. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A young adult has painful vision loss with impaired colour vision. What must be excluded?","choices":["Glaucoma","Retinoblastoma","Presbyopia","Multiple sclerosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Optic neuritis is a red flag for it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has her discs examined for glaucoma. Which change defines the damage?","choices":["Drusen at the macula","A cherry-red spot","Cupping, an excavated disc","Blurred disc margins with engorged veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a raised cup-to-disc ratio. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has bilateral tan iris nodules and multiple café-au-lait patches. What do the nodules represent?","choices":["Melanocytoma","Lisch nodules of neurofibromatosis type 1","Iris melanoma","Iris freckles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, bilateral, multifocal and non-progressive. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a normal brain scan and persistent papilledema. Which finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["An elevated opening pressure on lumbar puncture","An elevated intraocular pressure","A raised systemic blood pressure","A mass seen on magnetic resonance imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the diagnostic measurement. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who smokes asks about the older eye vitamin. Why was high-dose beta carotene dropped?","choices":["It stained the lens brown","It raised intraocular pressure","It interfered with anti-VEGF injections","It raised lung cancer risk in smokers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why AREDS2 replaced it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man is told he has the commonest eye cancer in adults. Which is it?","choices":["Uveal melanoma","Retinoblastoma","Conjunctival melanoma","Choroidal haemangioma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, arising from melanocytes of the choroid, ciliary body or iris. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man is told he has glaucoma. Which form is most common?","choices":["Congenital","Neovascular","Primary open-angle","Acute angle-closure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is characteristically painless and slow. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has a dense cataract. What is seen on ophthalmoscopy?","choices":["Disc cupping","A cherry-red spot","Flame haemorrhages","Loss of the red reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and a dense lens makes the pupil look grey or white. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Four patients present with headache. Which one fits idiopathic intracranial hypertension best?","choices":["A pregnant woman in the third trimester","A man with poorly controlled diabetes","An overweight woman of childbearing age","A thin man in his seventies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That demographic dominates the condition. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman has macular changes but no bleeding. Which findings define the dry form?","choices":["Disc cupping","A cherry-red spot","Leukocoria","Drusen, pigment change and atrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neovascular bleeding defines the wet form instead. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has ptosis, a dilated pupil and an eye turned down and out. Which nerve is involved?","choices":["Cranial nerve three","Cranial nerve four","Cranial nerve two","Cranial nerve seven"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pupil involvement makes it urgent. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with suspected idiopathic intracranial hypertension has a dilated exam. Which sign is expected?","choices":["Leukocoria","Papilledema","Cupping of the optic disc","A central scotoma with distortion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside visual field loss and a possible sixth nerve palsy. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which examination should a primary care clinician perform on every infant?","choices":["Slit lamp examination","The red reflex","Perimetry","Fluorescein angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, to catch leukocoria and strabismus early. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a raised vascular pigmented lesion on the conjunctiva. Which favours melanoma over a nevus?","choices":["It is bilateral","It is raised and vascular","It contains small clear cysts","It has been present since childhood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A conjunctival nevus is flat and often has clear cysts. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl is assessed for suspected strabismus. Which test elicits fixation of the misaligned eye?","choices":["Tonometry","The swinging flashlight test","Fluorescein staining","The cover test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the corneal light reflex is displaced in larger deviations. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with dry macular degeneration wants to watch for change at home. Which tool is used?","choices":["An Amsler grid","A Snellen chart","An ophthalmoscope","A pinhole occluder"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside serial slit lamp review. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"By what age should every child be screened for amblyopia?","choices":["Under five years","Under two years","Under ten years","At school entry only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, per the preventive task force and the paediatric academy. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has months of blurring and glare from oncoming headlights. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Amblyopia","Cataract","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow blurring with glare is the pattern. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A newborn has a dense unilateral cataract. Why is surgery not deferred?","choices":["To prevent amblyopia developing","Because the lens will rupture","To lower intraocular pressure","Because it will otherwise become malignant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The visual system will not wait. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has brown discoloration of the central lens and worse distance than near vision. Which cataract is this?","choices":["Zonular","Nuclear","Cortical","Posterior subcapsular"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the myopic shift is why near vision holds up. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has amblyopia of the left eye. What is the treatment?","choices":["Patch the amblyopic left eye","Anti-VEGF injection","Acetazolamide","Patch or blur the good right eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Forcing the weaker eye to work is the point. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has ptosis and diplopia that vary through the day. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Thyroid eye disease","Amblyopia","Retinoblastoma","Myasthenia gravis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is variable and mimics any strabismus pattern. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy cannot read the board but reads a book easily. Which lens corrects him?","choices":["A prism","No lens can correct it","A concave lens of negative power","A toric lens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It scatters light and moves focus back. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A mother notices one pupil looks white in photographs of her 2-year-old. What must be excluded?","choices":["Refractive error","Retinoblastoma","Amblyopia","Strabismus alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leukocoria is retinoblastoma until proven otherwise. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
